OpenWrt configuration export transport (2026-09-25)
=================================================

The OpenWrt client historically tried rpc-sys.backup. That method is absent from
the inspected stock rpcd implementation. LuCI instead posts the ubus session ID
as application/x-www-form-urlencoded data to /cgi-bin/cgi-backup. Its cgi-io
handler checks the cgi-io/backup/read ACL and streams sysupgrade --create-backup -.

This correction uses that CGI transport, with the existing session refresh and
TLS configuration. It refuses redirects, never puts the session in the URL, and
never falls back to the old RPC or a shell-command endpoint. A 401/403 is an
authentication error; other unsuccessful responses fail without provider text.
The adapter returns a fixed download error without configuration or diagnostics.
Cancellation propagates and closes the response. The total configured timeout
bounds session refresh and download, including a slow body that would otherwise
keep satisfying HTTPX per-read timeouts.

Compressed input is streamed with a 16 MiB ceiling. Invalid content lengths,
unexpected encodings/types and length mismatches are rejected. Gzip integrity is
checked with a separate 64 MiB inflated-data ceiling, discarding decoded chunks
without extracting files. This checks compressed-container integrity; it does not
validate every tar entry, prove completeness of a device's selected files or prove
that the archive can be restored on any device. Validation runs outside the event
loop; cancellation may leave that already-bounded in-memory validation finishing.

Sysupgrade may include configured files beyond /etc/config/. Export metadata now
says scope=sysupgrade. The delivery API and browser explicitly accept that value
and the older /etc/config/ value, preserving existing integrations. Other scopes
remain rejected. The API's permission checks, base64 encoding and 16 MiB payload
ceiling are unchanged. The client method retains its historic create_backup name
for adapter compatibility; the adapter's saved-backup creation remains unsupported.
No backup history entry, local artifact, retention job or restore is created here.

Dependencies and acceptance limits
---------------------------------
The gateway needs the LuCI/cgi-io backup endpoint and the session's backup read ACL.
Devices with ubus only or a nonstandard CGI base path are not covered. Existing TLS
policy still applies; this change does not qualify all network/TLS configurations.
At the initial transport qualification, the separate OpenWrt page had no export
button. Those browser checks exercised the generic gateway path. The subsequent
dedicated-page control and its separate checks are described in OPENWRT-EXPORT-UI.

Upstream references (captured with source hashes in private qualification evidence):
https://github.com/openwrt/luci/blob/f4f91aee257bab4eb9c6b7de6160cea294217956/modules/luci-mod-system/htdocs/luci-static/resources/view/system/flash.js
https://github.com/openwrt/cgi-io/blob/31cb3c89f02d918d7f17bf62a80c852fc38a1ca1/main.c

New regression coverage includes 68 transport cases (64 old failures, four controls)
and four API metadata cases (two old failures, two controls). The initial related
PostgreSQL run found two API failures after changing the metadata; that original
run is retained. The API and browser allowlists were then updated explicitly,
with new compatibility controls. Final results are recorded in the source-bound
qualification receipts, not inferred from the old run.

Independent loopback HTTPS checks use real HTTPX, certificate verification, a
synthetic ubus login and an owned CGI server. They reproduce the old method-not-found
behavior, check lossless exports and refusal cases, and verify that an untrusted
certificate receives no login request. The synthetic protocol peer is not an
OpenWrt appliance. Physical-device authentication, installed-package compatibility,
archive restore, backup durability, RTO/RPO and enterprise acceptance remain open.
